1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,840
Two travel websites. Two separate origins.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:09,020
One assistant that can cross between them,
but only with your permission.

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,080
Start on Skyline Air.

4
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:15,880
Pick a flight, enter a passenger, and book it.

5
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:22,900
The page holds the state, issues the reference,
and never leaves the browser.

6
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,120
Now ask the concierge about a hotel.

7
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:36,180
The model wants to call Harbor Hotel,
a different origin published by a different site.

8
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:40,360
This take uses a scripted model endpoint,
so the handoff is reproducible.

9
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:44,260
The same page runs Otari with a key.

10
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:48,600
Before anything leaves the page, you see who will be contacted,
why, and the exact request.

11
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:49,760
Decline.

12
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,740
The runtime stops the call.

13
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:56,240
Zero requests reach the hotel,
and the assistant tells you so.

14
00:00:57,900 --> 00:00:59,360
Ask again, and allow.

15
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:12,600
The runtime fetches the hotel's published manifest,
checks its policy, and runs its network.

16
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:23,760
Harbor Hotel receives the request,
not your chat history, and not your model key.

17
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:26,140
The trace shows every hop.

18
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:28,540
Origin, peer, consent, and result.

19
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:33,060
Fourteen tests cover the runtime and the boundary.

20
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:36,420
Trip Relay. Two sites, one visible boundary.

21
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:40,020
Live on GitHub Pages. MIT licensed.

22
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:45,260
Built on the MVP Profile,
with Mozilla Otari as the default router.
